 "transcript": {
  "answer": "I can't give you an AUD-to-peso rate from my sources. The Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA) publishes about 25 exchange rates against the Australian dollar, and the Argentine peso isn't one of them.\n\n**What I can tell you:**\n- On **16 October 2026** (the latest RBA figure, at 4pm Sydney time), **A$1 bought US$0.6947**.\n- So far in October 2026 it has stayed between about US$0.693 and US$0.700.\n\n**To work out the peso amount:**\n1. Look up how many pesos one US dollar buys today, from a bank or a site like XE.\n2. Multiply that number by 0.6947.\n\nFor example, if US$1 buys 1,400 pesos, then A$1 buys about 1,400 × 0.6947 ≈ 973 pesos. That's just an example, not the real rate.\n\n**Something to watch for:** Argentina often has more than one exchange rate. The official rate can be quite different from the \"blue\" (informal) rate and the \"MEP\" or card rates. The rate you actually get depends on how you change the money, such as cash, card or a transfer service.",
